@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
The scales or *tropes* of the best Greek period have hitherto been considered as essential, that is the lowest tone or *hypatē* has been considered as the tonic. But I cannot find any definite ground for this assumption. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
What Aristotle says, as we have seen, makes the middle tone or *mesē*, function as the tonic, but yet it cannot be denied that other attributes of our tonic belong to the *hypatē*.⁎ Whatever may have been the real state of the case, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
whether the *mesē* or *hypatē* be regarded as the tonic, whether the scales be considered as all authentic or all plagal, it is extremely probable that the Greeks, among whom we first find the complete diatonic scale, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
took the liberty of using every tone of this scale as a tonic, just as we have seen that every one of the five tones forming the scales of the Chinese and Gaels occasionally functions as a tonic. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
The same scales are also found, probably handed down immediately by ancient tradition, in the ancient Christian ecclesiastical music.

@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
Hence if we disregard the chromatic and enharmonic scales, and the apparently arbitrary scales of the Asiatics, none of which have shewn themselves capable of ¶ further development,† homophonic vocal music developed seven diatonic scales, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
which differ from one another in about the same way as our major and minor scales. These differences will be better appreciated by making them all begin with the same tonic *c*.‡

@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
Footnote+ seen that this paragraph materially alters the intonation from what would result from a mere beginning of each mode with a different note of the Pythagorean or diatonic scale. @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
I therefore repeat the scales as defined by this paragraph in the notation explained on pp. 276a to 277a and note⁎, and write between each pair of notes the number of cents in the interval between each pair of notes, @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
which will be found useful in future comparisons. These scales ¶ should be traced out on the Duodenarium, App. XX. sect. E. art. 18. They cannot be played on the Harmonical.
@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
